  "unit": "together-against",
  "title": "Together: con-, com- and syn-",
  "short": "con- (Latin) and syn- (Greek) mean with or together.",
  "trouble": "con- changes to com-, col-, cor- and co- to fit the next sound; syn- changes to sym- and syl-. Sometimes con- only strengthens a word, meaning thoroughly (confound).",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.ex-in",
    "roots.latin-greek"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "To <i>connect</i> is to tie together, to <i>combine</i> is to put together and to <i>collect</i> is to gather together. The Latin prefix <b>con-</b> means “with” or “together,” and it is the most common prefix on the list: thirty-six words use it. Greek has a partner, <b>syn-</b>, in <i>synonym</i> (a word with the same meaning) and <i>symphony</i> (sounds together)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
